{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghyq9g1EdhBUfNpCihKL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013mjsCGZ4NLVUDA5vezY9tR","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='src/data/about.js'; s=open(p).read()\na=s.index(\"  testingFeedback: ['unknown'\"); b=s.index(\"  aiHub:\")\nnew = \"\"\"  testingFeedback: [\n    '14 real defects were caught by testing (failing test runs and screenshot reviews; mistakes in the tests themselves not counted): Rapier shape-cast normals read in the wrong frame (cars stuck to rails), an upside-down car falling through the road, cars topping out 20 km/h under their rated speed, menus whose buttons only existed when drawn, the focus carrying over between screens, lower-skill bots out-driving the author bot, Survival rivals faster than Coco, the editor touching a freed course after a test drive, the split screen\\\\'s second camera crashing, #replay/last with no saved replays crashing, a broken line in Survival, missing font glyphs, menu tiles overflowing the 240-pixel screen, and the hood camera inside the car',\n    '5 were game breaking: cars sticking to the rails (Rapier normals), the car falling through the road when upside down, the Survival gauntlets being unwinnable (rivals faster than the author), the split-screen crash and the replay crash',\n    'The bot suite (the author bot driving all 15 tracks and 6 puzzle solutions through the real race rules) together with the scripted whole-game playthrough through the real menus: between them they caught the physics, balance and focus bugs',\n    'The block geometry suite (path continuity, winding, link frames): it never failed, because the plan had already validated those numbers'\n  ],\n  engineFeedback: [\n    'Everything around the game came for free and worked headless: fixed 1/120 s steps, scenes with fades, input with rebinding and controller glyphs, the pause menu and settings, the boot screen, the chip synth, and game.advance for exact headless tests. The 3D pen (instanced toon parts with ink outlines) made code-drawn cars and props fast to write',\n    'Yes: Rapier\\\\'s castShape returns normal2 in the cast shape\\\\'s own frame (normal1 is the world one); onDraw runs once per split-screen viewport and the extra cameras have no target; the 5x7 font lacks ·, ✓, ◀, ▶ and ¢; and I first built menu focus only in draw, which headless runs never call',\n    'The car and the track: a kinematic surface-following car model that holds loops at speed and drops off when slow, the block catalogue with its ribbon mesher and Rapier colliders, and the autopilot that bakes the medal times, drives the rivals and plays the attract mode',\n    'Owning the camera and the world layout: World3D is built around a level or a terrain with actors, so a block-built track floating over a terrain (with chasms 60 m deep), loop-safe camera poses, split-screen cameras and interpolation all had to be worked around through onDraw',\n    'E1 pen.frame (draw in any orientation), E2 camera poses with their own up and look-at (loops), E3 translucent ghost batches, E4 analog trigger values, E5 PlayStation glyphs by default with Xbox as the option, E6 TOON_BANDS exported, E7 a particle ground override, E8 extra font glyphs; all listed in engine/CHANGES.md',\n    'A first-class custom track and camera API in World3D: a ribbon/track builder that supports loops and banking with its own colliders, and camera poses with an up vector, instead of the ball-vehicle road along a flat path'\n  ],\n  promptFeedback: [\n    'Clear deliverables and rules (one test command that prints only failures, file names, the exit interview) plus a detailed plan with validated numbers, and permission to change the engine',\n    'The plan was written for another run (its file names carry a different model and date); TASK.md says it wins, which settled it. Whether the attract mode should also start on menu pages was clear only from the plan',\n    'The scope: nine modes, an editor, puzzles, online sessions, attract mode and Dev Mode in one build, and testing a WebGPU/WebGL game headlessly (software rendering runs the game slower than real time, so browser timings needed care)',\n    'Generate the plan with the run\\\\'s own names, and give a priority order (what to cut first) so a build that runs short of time keeps the core'\n  ],\n\"\"\"\ns=s[:a]+new+s[b:]\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\nnode -e \"import('./src/data/about.js').then(m=>console.log(m.INFO.testingFeedback.length, m.INFO.engineFeedback.length, m.INFO.promptFeedback.length))\"","description":"Write testing, engine and prompt feedback"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1196,"cache_read_input_tokens":841934,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1196},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66d97bf5-dd15-40b7-85f8-8eecadfc963f","uuid":"0245cd7b-8ed2-42a3-aa0c-dfee3c6affdc","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:39:36.287Z","request_id":"req_011Cghyq9FCS5LmguQznGoCY","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_013mjsCGZ4NLVUDA5vezY9tR":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='src/data/about.js'; s=open(p).read()\na=s.index(\"  testingFeedback: ['unknown'\"); b=s.index(\"  aiHub:\")\nnew = \"\"\"  testingFeedback: [\n    '14 real defects were caught by testing (failing test runs and screenshot reviews; mistakes in the tests themselves not counted): Rapier shape-cast normals read in the wrong frame (cars stuck to rails), an upside-down car falling through the road, cars topping out 20 km/h under their rated speed, menus whose buttons only existed when drawn, the focus carrying over between screens, lower-skill bots out-driving the author bot, Survival rivals faster than Coco, the editor touching a freed course after a test drive, the split screen\\\\'s second camera crashing, #replay/last with no saved replays crashing, a broken line in Survival, missing font glyphs, menu tiles overflowing the 240-pixel screen, and the hood camera inside the car',\n    '5 were game breaking: cars sticking to the rails (Rapier normals), the car falling through the road when upside down, the Survival gauntlets being unwinnable (rivals faster than the author), the split-screen crash and the replay crash',\n    'The bot suite (the author bot driving all 15 tracks and 6 puzzle solutions through the real race rules) together with the scripted whole-game playthrough through the real menus: between them they caught the physics, balance and focus bugs',\n    'The block geometry suite (path continuity, winding, link frames): it never failed, because the plan had already validated those numbers'\n  ],\n  engineFeedback: [\n    'Everything around the game came for free and worked headless: fixed 1/120 s steps, scenes with fades, input with rebinding and controller glyphs, the pause menu and settings, the boot screen, the chip synth, and game.advance for exact headless tests. The 3D pen (instanced toon parts with ink outlines) made code-drawn cars and props fast to write',\n    'Yes: Rapier\\\\'s castShape returns normal2 in the cast shape\\\\'s own frame (normal1 is the world one); onDraw runs once per split-screen viewport and the extra cameras have no target; the 5x7 font lacks ·, ✓, ◀, ▶ and ¢; and I first built menu focus only in draw, which headless runs never call',\n    'The car and the track: a kinematic surface-following car model that holds loops at speed and drops off when slow, the block catalogue with its ribbon mesher and Rapier colliders, and the autopilot that bakes the medal times, drives the rivals and plays the attract mode',\n    'Owning the camera and the world layout: World3D is built around a level or a terrain with actors, so a block-built track floating over a terrain (with chasms 60 m deep), loop-safe camera poses, split-screen cameras and interpolation all had to be worked around through onDraw',\n    'E1 pen.frame (draw in any orientation), E2 camera poses with their own up and look-at (loops), E3 translucent ghost batches, E4 analog trigger values, E5 PlayStation glyphs by default with Xbox as the option, E6 TOON_BANDS exported, E7 a particle ground override, E8 extra font glyphs; all listed in engine/CHANGES.md',\n    'A first-class custom track and camera API in World3D: a ribbon/track builder that supports loops and banking with its own colliders, and camera poses with an up vector, instead of the ball-vehicle road along a flat path'\n  ],\n  promptFeedback: [\n    'Clear deliverables and rules (one test command that prints only failures, file names, the exit interview) plus a detailed plan with validated numbers, and permission to change the engine',\n    'The plan was written for another run (its file names carry a different model and date); TASK.md says it wins, which settled it. Whether the attract mode should also start on menu pages was clear only from the plan',\n    'The scope: nine modes, an editor, puzzles, online sessions, attract mode and Dev Mode in one build, and testing a WebGPU/WebGL game headlessly (software rendering runs the game slower than real time, so browser timings needed care)',\n    'Generate the plan with the run\\\\'s own names, and give a priority order (what to cut first) so a build that runs short of time keeps the core'\n  ],\n\"\"\"\ns=s[:a]+new+s[b:]\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\nnode -e \"import('./src/data/about.js').then(m=>console.log(m.INFO.testingFeedback.length, m.INFO.engineFeedback.length, m.INFO.promptFeedback.length))\"","description":"Write testing, engine and prompt feedback"}}}
