{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghzyDmu4EAXwzKWPgve5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I built **CRACKMANIA** (the title the plan picked) from TASK.md and PLAN.md. It's an arcade time-attack stunt racer on block-built tracks, with loops, kickers over canyons, banked bends and boost pads. You race the clock and a ghost of your best run, earn medals, and spend coppers on track pieces.\n\nAll test runs pass on the final code: 706 headless checks, 39 browser checks of the built file, and all three AI Hub harness runs. After the last harness run I changed only About/credits text and rebuilt.\n\n- **Game file:** `Crackmania-TrackMania2003-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050308.html` (5 MB, opens from `file://`, everything inlined).\n- **Exit interview:** `exit-interview.json`, beside the game; it parses and every field is filled.\n- **Dev folder:** `Crackmania-TrackMania2003-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050308/`. `README.md` has the build and test commands; `engine/CHANGES.md` lists the engine edits.\n\n**What's in it:**\n- **Race:** 15 tracks across Dustbowl, Hedgerow and Frostpeak, each with its own car. Bronze, silver, gold and \"Coco's Time\" medals, unlocks and story beats.\n- **Puzzle:** 6 puzzles solved in the editor.\n- **Survival:** a 5-round gauntlet per environment against the three \"Ghost Guild\" rivals.\n- **Build:** the full editor, with undo/redo, validate-by-driving, 6 save slots and share codes.\n- **Multiplayer:** Hot Seat (2–8 players), Split Screen (2–4), and Online as AI Hub ghost-mode sessions with the game's own card.\n- **Also:** Replays with TV cameras, a Garage paint shop, Dev Mode from the pause menu (tools, live tuning sliders with reset, info), a ~100-second attract mode, the touch-only gate, and About/Credits.\n- **Controls:** controller-first, PlayStation glyphs by default with Xbox as a setting, analog triggers, mouse hover and click in menus.\n- **Audio:** all music, effects and gibberish voices are generated in code.\n\n**Things you might notice:**\n- Medal times come from a bot that drives each track; when the same bot drives as the player it usually lands just under Coco's time and earns gold, so the tests require gold or better.\n- In split screen, the top view showed less distant scenery than the bottom one in my check. I didn't track down why; racing in it works.\n- In software rendering the game runs at roughly a third of real speed, so the browser checks wait on game state rather than fixed delays.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:03:08\n- **Source:** TrackMania (2003, Nadeo)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade racing (time attack) with a track-building puzzle mode. Third-person chase camera (far, close, hood) in races; an orbiting three-quarter camera in the editor and puzzles; trackside TV cameras in replays.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2. Its 2D engine runs the game (steps, scenes, input, menus, settings, audio, rigs, particles, UI); its 3D engine draws the worlds. three.js r182 (WebGPU renderer, falls back to WebGL 2). Rapier 0.19.3 for ray and shape casts; the car is a custom kinematic model. AI Hub session client 1.4.0. Built with esbuild 0.25; tested with Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 (Chromium).\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native low-resolution toon 3D with ink outlines and pixel-art textures baked in code. Saturated trackside colour: red-and-white curbs, chunky gates with dot-matrix boards, chevron boost pads. Three environments: desert, countryside, alpine dusk.\n- **Animation:** The engine's rigs and poses (crowd, Monsieur Drapeau's flag routine, dialog portraits) and its tweens. Code-driven: suspension, wheel spin and steering, body lean, flames, roll-overs, windmills, pumpjacks, cows, tumbleweeds, start lights, confetti, fireworks, and blocks dropping in.\n- **Audio:** Eight synthesized songs in layers, medal and event stings, a WebAudio engine sound per car with a five-speed gearbox and tyre, wind and scrape noise, UI and race effects, and Animalese-style gibberish voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode: up to 4 players, a 3-minute time attack, each other shown as ghost cars. Locally: hot seat and split screen.\n- **Testing:** One command. 706 headless checks in 15 suites, including a scripted whole-game playthrough through the real menus. 39 Playwright checks of the built file. The AI Hub harness: 2 players; 4 players with latency, jitter and loss; and the host leaving. Testing was unrestricted.\n- **Research:** No web research during the build; TrackMania facts came from PLAN.md. I read the engine and AI Hub repositories (manuals, API card, protocol README, harness).\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** Cost and token counts: unknown (not visible to me). Session time: about 1 hour 55 minutes.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 14 real defects (test-side mistakes not counted):\n  - Rapier shape-cast normals read in the wrong frame, so cars stuck to the rails.\n  - An upside-down car fell through the road.\n  - Cars topped out 20 km/h under their rated speed.\n  - Menu buttons only existed once drawn, so key navigation failed headless.\n  - The focused button carried over from one screen to the next.\n  - Lower-skill bots out-drove the author bot.\n  - Survival rivals were faster than Coco's own time.\n  - The editor touched freed track physics after a test drive.\n  - Split screen's second camera crashed drawing.\n  - `#replay/last` crashed when no replays were saved.\n  - A line in Survival broke during an edit.\n  - The pixel font was missing several glyphs.\n  - Menu tiles overflowed the 240-pixel-tall screen.\n  - The hood camera sat inside the car body.\n- **How many would be considered critical / game breaking?** 5: cars sticking to rails, falling through the road, unwinnable Survival gauntlets, the split-screen crash and the replay crash.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The bot suite (the author bot driving all 15 tracks and 6 puzzles under the real race rules) together with the whole-game playthrough.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The block-geometry suite. It never failed, because the plan had already validated those numbers.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** Everything around the game came free and worked headless: fixed steps, scenes and fades, input with rebinding and glyphs, the pause menu and settings, the boot screen, the synth, and `game.advance` for exact tests. The 3D pen (instanced toon parts with outlines) made code-drawn cars and props quick to write.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n  - Rapier's `castShape` gives `normal2` in the cast shape's own frame; `normal1` is the world-space one.\n  - `onDraw` runs once per split-screen viewport, and added cameras start without a target.\n  - The 5x7 font lacks · ✓ ◀ ▶ and ¢.\n  - I first built menu focus only in draw, which headless runs never call.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The kinematic car that holds loops at speed and falls off when slow; the block catalogue with its ribbon mesher and colliders; and the bot that sets medal times, drives rivals and plays the attract mode.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Taking over the camera and the world layout. World3D is built around a level or terrain with actors, so a block track floating over a terrain (with 60 m chasms), loop-safe camera poses and split-screen cameras all went through `onDraw` workarounds.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** Eight changes, listed in `engine/CHANGES.md`:\n  - E1: `pen.frame`, to draw a car in any orientation.\n  - E2: camera poses with their own up vector and look-at point, for loops.\n  - E3: translucent ghost-car batches.\n  - E4: analog trigger values.\n  - E5: PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox as the option.\n  - E6: the toon bands exported for track materials.\n  - E7: a particle ground override.\n  - E8: the missing font glyphs.\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** A first-class custom-track and camera API: a ribbon builder that supports loops and banking with its own colliders, and camera poses with an up vector.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** Clear deliverables and rules (one failures-only test command, exact file names, the exit interview), a detailed plan with validated numbers, and permission to change the engine.\n- **What was confusing?** The plan was written for another run, so its file names carry a different model and date; \"TASK.md wins\" settled it. That the attract mode should also start on menu pages was clear only from the plan.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** The scope (nine modes, an editor, puzzles, online, attract mode and Dev Mode in one build), and testing a WebGPU/WebGL game headlessly, where software rendering runs slower than real time.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Generate the plan with the run's own names, and give a priority order of what to cut first if time runs short."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":451,"cache_read_input_tokens":876138,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":451},"output_tokens":802,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66d97bf5-dd15-40b7-85f8-8eecadfc963f","uuid":"e8d57d0b-c3e9-4200-91b4-0c4663a5e87d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:54:19.028Z","request_id":"req_011CghzyDQ5PXXTRJjnLFkmH"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Not all four requirements are shown satisfied. The bundled HTML and exit-interview.json exist and the final reply includes all four sections, but the transcript does not show that every TASK.md feature is implemented without stubs. Several features were built in reduced form or left partly unverified: the Build editor's 'validate' and 'share code' were exercised, but the Replays browser check failed earlier and was only re-run after fixes, and the Online and touch-only paths were checked only with fakes or single screenshots. The transcript also records unresolved issues the agent itself reported: split screen's top view showed less distant scenery than the bottom one, and the 'every feature with no placeholders' claim was never checked by a scan that covered all TASK.md items (only a grep for TODO/placeholder/stub markers, which returned nothing). Also the exit-interview answers were written as the agent's own feedback without a per-feature audit."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66d97bf5-dd15-40b7-85f8-8eecadfc963f","uuid":"9d7f1f6f-f3aa-42bc-8833-1af17462e8d1","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:54:47.903Z","isSynthetic":true}
